window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Must Know Quick Reference","secs":[{"s":"Must Know Quick Reference","a":"","k":["Exam Prep","Everything the instructor explicitly said would be tested or heavily hinted. A ★ means it was said out loud."]},{"s":"Week 1▾","a":"week-1","k":["Week 1▾","Topic Name","★ A short, quick-reference bullet (about 20 words).","An unstarred bullet keeps its attribution: she hinted this would show up.","Not Tested","Record every \"you don't need to memorize this\" the moment it's said."]}]},{"f":"week1-comprehensive-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1","Part 1 — Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics","Part 2 — CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome","Part 3 — Pericarditis","Part 4 — Infective Endocarditis","Part 5 — Valvular Heart Disease","Self-Test Flashcards","Everything covered in Week 1, in one long page, in the order it was taught: Part 1 cardiovascular labs and diagnostics, Part 2 CAD and acute coronary syndrome, Part 3 pericarditis, Part 4 infective endocarditis, and Part 5 valvular heart di…"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics▾","a":"part-1-cardiovascular-labs-diagnostics","k":["Part 1 — Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics▾","From Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics. Built from the two Week 1 labs-and-diagnostics voiceovers: serum labs and non-invasive tests (part 1), then the invasive procedures (part 2). The instructor says you will not need to memorize the nume…","Serum Cardiac Markers","When a patient has a myocardial infarction (MI), there is heart cell death, and the markers below are what the blood draw shows.","Troponin — The most sensitive indicator of myocardial damage. Helps differentiate cardiac from non-cardiac chest pain (for example, cardiac versus GI substernal pressure). — Elevates 2 to 3 hours after the MI; returns to baseline in 10 to 1…","CK-MB — Muscle damage, but not specific to the heart. — Elevated 3 hours after an MI; stays elevated 24 hours to 2 days. — Historically important and used before troponin existed; \"NCLEX still likes to talk about it.\" Not as specific as tro…","Myoglobin — Another non-specific marker of myocardial damage (less specific than troponin). — Not given. — Mentioned only because it shows up on some standardized tests. The instructor says you don't need to know myoglobin.","Inflammation Markers: CRP & ESR","CRP (C-reactive protein)","Indicates inflammation in the body; non-specific (it doesn't say where).","More sensitive than the ESR.","Peaks within 18 to 72 hours after an MI; failure to normalize might indicate ongoing damage to the heart muscle.","With an MI there is inflammation in addition to heart cell death.","Monitors more closely for the acute picture.","ESR (sed rate)","Also a non-specific indicator of inflammation; could possibly be infection.","More useful for monitoring chronic inflammation.","CRP and ESR often go hand in hand.","Neither tells you where in the body the inflammation is (it could be an arthritis flare, for instance).","BNP & Pro-BNP","Brain natriuretic peptide","Despite the word \"brain,\" the main source of BNP is the ventricles of the heart.","Helps distinguish cardiac versus respiratory causes of dyspnea: a short-of-breath patient in the ED, where the provider wants to know if it is heart failure or respiratory failure.","An elevated BNP indicates left ventricular dysfunction.","Pro-BNP is what the instructor sees most often at UK. Plasma concentrations of BNP and pro-BNP are similar, but with left ventricular dysfunction (left-sided heart failure) pro-BNP rises much more significantly than BNP.","No ranges given: you don't need to memorize numbers for this, and NCLEX won't expect you to.","Rule of thumb from the lecture: BNP or pro-BNP elevated, think heart failure. Troponin elevated, think myocardial infarction.","Cholesterol & Lipoproteins","Total cholesterol","The main lipid associated with cardiovascular disease.","We eat it and we also manufacture it; high cholesterol can be inherited (familial).","Done as part of a lipid profile; not a totally accurate predictor of heart disease by itself.","Diurnal variation: a morning reading can differ from an afternoon reading.","Affected by malnutrition: if it is very low, also look at the serum albumin to see how malnourished the patient is.","HDL and LDL","An accurate predictor of heart disease.","HDL = the \"good\" cholesterol; removes cholesterol in the body.","LDL = the \"bad\" cholesterol; deposits cholesterol in the peripheral tissue.","We like high HDLs and low LDLs.","The values help determine whether the patient is low, moderate, or high risk for CAD.","The one number to know: total cholesterol less than 200. The instructor does not ask you to memorize the LDL, HDL, or triglyceride numbers; a test question would speak in general terms.","Non-Invasive Procedures","12-lead EKG (ECG) — Looks at the heart's conduction system from 12 different perspectives simultaneously. A snapshot in time (takes 2 to 3 minutes at the bedside). — The provider can diagnose an MI with a 12-lead. Nurses and nursing student…","Telemetry (\"tele\") — Ongoing, continuous monitoring of the cardiac rhythm (telebox around the neck, chest electrodes, or bedside monitoring; may be watched at a central monitoring area). — Only a one-way perspective of the heart's rhythm. I…","Ambulatory EKG (Holter monitor) — Like a 24-hour EKG, worn at home to capture heart activity, for example with syncope of unknown origin that could be a dysrhythmia. — The patient keeps a diary (exercise, anything that feels funny in the he…","Stress test — Evaluates the heart's response to exercise (treadmill or exercise bike), stressing the heart on purpose. — For chest pain with exertion but not at rest. CAD may show minimal symptoms and a normal resting EKG; exercise unmasks …","Echocardiogram (\"echo\") — Uses sound waves to create a moving picture of the heart. — Evaluates the valves, chamber size, and time in diastole and systole; measures ejection fraction. No radiation, no prep, painless. Bedside or outpatient; …","12-Lead EKG vs Telemetry","12-lead EKG","12 perspectives at once.","A snapshot in time.","Can diagnose an MI.","Telemetry","A one-way perspective.","Continuous and ongoing.","Monitors only; cannot diagnose.","Ejection Fraction","Definition and values","The amount of blood pumped out of the left ventricle with each contraction; an indication of how effectively the heart is pumping.","Measured with an echocardiogram, and (per the lecture) also with a right-sided heart cath.","Normal: 55% to 70% of the blood sitting in the ventricle at the end of diastole is ejected (about half or a little more).","Low values, such as 40%, are seen in heart failure. A really, really low ejection fraction can mean the patient might need a heart transplant.","Very important information for a patient who might have heart failure.","Invasive Procedures","TEE (transesophageal echocardiogram) — A probe is inserted into the esophagus. The plain echo shows the anterior heart; the TEE shows the posterior heart and can give a lot more information. Done in the vascular lab. — Contrast dye may be u…","EP study (electrophysiology study) — Evaluates the electrical health of the heart, usually for dysrhythmias not responding to standard treatment. Dysrhythmias are induced on purpose in a controlled environment to confirm the root of the ele…","Cardiac catheterization with angiography (\"heart cath\"; some older patients call it a \"dye test\") — Dye plus pictures. Gives information about the heart muscle, the coronary arteries, and the valves. Diagnostic: confirms the location and ex…","Left vs Right Heart Cath","Left heart cath","Enter an artery (femoral, radial, or brachial).","The catheter goes retrograde up into the ascending aorta / arch of the aorta.","The goal is always to get to the coronary arteries; this is the absolute only way to reach them.","Used when a patient has chest pain or angina and the question is coronary occlusion.","Places examined: the aorta, right coronary artery, left anterior descending, circumflex, and left main coronary artery; the left ventricle sits at the apex.","Right heart cath","Enter the venous system.","Can measure ejection fraction and look at the pressures of the right side of the heart.","Cannot look at the coronary arteries.","Sometimes only one side is done; a lot of times both are done.","Before the Heart Cath","Pre-procedure","NPO; meds with sips of water may be allowed (provider decision).","Signed consent (definitely invasive); the patient and family need to understand the procedure and what to expect.","Very little pain, because blood vessels don't have nerve fibers.","Warn the patient about the warm flush / hot flash when the contrast dye is injected; it is an expected finding, so they don't think something is wrong.","Pre-procedure checklist; monitor BUN and creatinine (dye can be nephrotoxic); empty the bladder before going.","Local anesthesia and likely IV sedation to keep them from being anxious.","After the Heart Cath","Recovery in the cath lab observation area before returning to the room — Standard recovery location.","Monitor clotting time closely before the sheath is pulled — Anticoagulants are used during the procedure and DC'd afterward; arteries are high-pressure vessels, so bleeding is possible (especially after a heparin drip).","Sheath removed, then pressure held up to about 20 minutes — A long time compared to a regular IV, because the vessel needs to seal.","Bed rest at least 8 hours if the femoral (groin) site was used; affected leg straight; head of bed below 30 degrees — So the vessel doesn't bleed.","Frequent assessment of circulation distal to the puncture: pulse, color, sensation — Make sure there is good perfusion on the other side of where the vessel was penetrated.","Potential Complications of a Heart Cath","Bleeding (number one) — An IV heparin drip is used during the procedure to decrease clot risk; monitor the puncture site closely for hematomas.","Emboli — Monitor for signs and symptoms of stroke.","Infection — A listed risk.","Dysrhythmias — A listed risk.","Damage to the heart — If the pericardium is accidentally poked, the patient may develop cardiac tamponade.","Allergy to the dye — Monitor for it.","Contrast nephrotoxicity — Risk is higher in kidney failure; see below.","Contrast Nephrotoxicity","Acute kidney injury after dye","An acute kidney injury (AKI) that happens after a dye injection; a major cause of AKI in the hospitalized patient.","Increased risk with existing renal problems, diabetes, or fluid volume deficit.","Acetylcysteine, and sometimes sodium bicarbonate, are given before the procedure to offset the elevated BUN and creatinine.","Encourage the patient to force fluids afterward to dilute the dye going through the kidneys."]},{"s":"Part 2 — CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome▾","a":"part-2-cad-acute-coronary-syndrome","k":["Part 2 — CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome▾","From CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome. Built from the three Week 1 coronary voiceovers: coronary heart disease and chronic stable angina (part 1), acute coronary syndrome and MI (part 2), and caring for the patient with ACS, including PCI, thr…","Coronary Arteries & Terminology","The basics","Coronary heart disease, coronary artery disease (CAD), and ischemic heart disease are used interchangeably.","The coronary arteries perfuse the heart, getting oxygen to the heart muscle. We want patent coronary arteries.","You can predict what area of the heart an artery perfuses from the anatomy. The instructor is not going to ask you to memorize the arteries, but the providers look at a few main ones: the left main, the left anterior descending (LAD), the c…","The left main and LAD perfuse the left ventricle, the powerhouse chamber. Complete blockage there is sometimes called the \"widow maker\", because those patients often never make it to the hospital.","Atherosclerosis (\"hardening of the arteries\") is responsible for almost all cases of CAD, regardless of type.","The CAD Continuum","Think of coronary artery disease as a continuum, from chronic ischemic heart disease to acute coronary syndrome (ACS). Once it starts looking like unstable angina, it is collectively called ACS, because time is of the essence if it advances…","Stable angina — Unstable angina — NSTEMI — STEMI","Chronic ischemic heart disease; the patient lives with it. — ACS. No heart damage. — ACS. Heart damage. — ACS. More extensive heart damage.","\"A STEMI is worse than a non-STEMI, a non-STEMI is worse than unstable angina, and unstable angina is worse than stable angina.\"","Risk Factors for CAD","Non-modifiable","Age: the older someone gets, the more likely CAD is.","Gender: males are at greater risk than females.","Family history: especially relatives who developed CAD younger.","The four most firmly established modifiable","Elevated serum lipids (cholesterol, triglycerides); we want high HDLs and low LDLs.","Hypertension: untreated, the increased arterial pressure causes damage over time (the hardening). It can be silent and asymptomatic, so people don't perceive it as a big deal. Patients with CKD or diabetes have an even lower BP goal.","Tobacco use: risk is proportional to the number of cigarettes smoked.","Physical inactivity.","Other modifiable risk factors","Obesity: apple-shaped people (rotund in the belly) are at higher risk than pear-shaped people (hips wider than the abdomen).","Diabetes mellitus: poor glucose control aggravates coronary heart disease.","Depression or prolonged stress.","Teaching for Each Modifiable Risk Factor","Elevated serum lipids — Monitor lipids regularly; diet control (low-fat diet); statin medications.","Hypertension — Check BP regularly; antihypertensive medications; DASH diet (low-salt, low-fat).","Tobacco — Don't start; if they smoke, encourage stopping. Switching to lower-nicotine or filtered cigarettes does not impact the risk. The benefits of cessation are dramatic and start almost immediately, even after 20 years of smoking.","Physical inactivity — Middle-aged patients or those with a history of medical problems should see the provider before starting. Exercise sessions at least 40 minutes long with a 10-minute warm-up and cool-down, moderately 3 to 4 times a wee…","Obesity — Avoid fad diets; restrict saturated fats, sweets, and high-cholesterol foods; increase physical activity.","Diabetes — Tight glucose control: glycemic index diet, carb counting, diabetes education, hemoglobin A1C checked regularly.","Stress — Incorporate stress-reduction activities.","Chronic Stable Angina","Angina means chest pain from a temporary loss of oxygenation to the heart muscle, more often than not from an occluded coronary artery (but you can't know without the tests).","Where the pain can be","Substernal, possibly radiating up to the neck.","The classic: substernal, radiating down the left arm. It can radiate down both arms.","Epigastric (troponins help rule cardiac in or out; sometimes it is just gastric reflux).","Mid-scapular. The instructor once had a patient with pain in the elbows: it is not exactly predictable.","What makes it \"stable\"","Occurs with moderate to prolonged exertion, in a pattern familiar to the patient.","Frequency, duration, and intensity stay pretty much stable.","Only slight limitations in activity; regulated on medication.","Usually associated with fixed atherosclerotic plaque.","Relieved by rest and/or nitroglycerin.","Managed with drug therapy; rarely requires aggressive therapy.","Diagnosing Stable Angina","Workup","Physical exam and complete history; EKG.","Exercise stress test, or a pharmacological stress test if they can't use the treadmill or bike: a drug with positive inotropic and chronotropic effects (increased contractility and heart rate) to stress the heart on purpose.","Low and intermediate risk results: manage symptoms without an intervention.","High risk results: more testing, such as cardiac catheterization followed by revascularization (PCI or CABG), and/or an echo or TEE.","Goals and Medications for Stable Angina","Goal: decrease the workload on the heart and increase the oxygen supply, targeting the modifiable risk factors as appropriate.","Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid), antiplatelet — 81 mg (the chewable) or 325 mg (the adult form); evidence doesn't show one works better. Anyone at risk for CAD needs to be taking an aspirin a day.","Lipid-lowering agents — Already covered in patho.","Beta blockers (\"-olol\") — The only classification proven to prevent re-infarction and improve survival for MI patients. Long-acting beta blockers are sometimes used.","Calcium channel blockers — Usually given in conjunction with the beta blocker.","Nitrates — Typically sublingual; first-line treatment for acute anginal symptoms. Long-acting nitrates also exist.","Sodium current inhibitors (Ranexa) — The newer therapy.","Nitroglycerin","Current guideline: if chest pain has not improved, or has worsened, 5 minutes after the first nitro, the patient calls 911 and chews an aspirin immediately (antiplatelet effects begin within an hour and continue for several days). The old t…","Sublingual (most common) — Rapid acting. Under the tongue; don't swallow. Repeat every 5 minutes times 3 if needed. At home, if one doesn't work, activate EMS. In the hospital, take vital signs before each dose; you might give it every 5 mi…","Translingual — Rapid acting; on the tongue.","IV — Rapid acting; dosed in micrograms per minute (med math). Comes in a glass bottle with special vented tubing.","Skin patch and ointment — Use gloves to apply and remove (it can vasodilate you and give you a horrendous headache). Never defibrillate over the patch.","Isosorbide — For preventing angina attacks; taken daily. Tolerance builds up over time.","Teaching about sublingual nitro","Take it at the beginning of chest pain, or prophylactically before activity known to cause it (walking, bike riding, sexual activity).","Works in less than 3 minutes; keep it accessible (in your pocket).","Protect it from light (brown bottle); replace the bottle every 6 months.","It should tingle as it dissolves; no tingle may mean it has expired. Continued pain or no tingle: activate EMS.","Never combine with erectile dysfunction drugs; this can be fatal.","Side effects (all from vasodilation)","Headache (not a reason to skip the nitro).","Lowered BP: fainting and postural hypotension.","Flushing.","Always know the blood pressure before giving nitroglycerin, because it is going to lower it. A patient with a low baseline (for example 90/60) can plummet. If the BP is too low, no more nitro is given; something different, like IV morphine,…","Acute Coronary Syndrome: Unstable Angina, NSTEMI & STEMI","ACS is what happens when the slippery slope begins and the patient has more signs and symptoms than we want. It is divided into unstable angina and acute MI.","Unstable angina — No changes (or ST depression / deep T-wave inversion) — Not elevated — None, but a sign to be vigilant about slipping into an NSTEMI or STEMI","NSTEMI (non-ST-elevation MI) — No ST elevation (ST depression / deep T-wave inversion, or no changes) — Elevated — Yes","STEMI (ST-elevation MI) — ST elevation — Elevated — Yes, more extensive","Unstable Angina","How it differs from stable angina","Often occurs at rest, unannounced and unprecipitated; usually lasts more than 20 minutes.","New onset that markedly limits physical activity (stops the person in their tracks).","Increasing angina: more frequent, longer, with less exertion than the patient's previous stable angina.","Poorly relieved by rest or nitroglycerin.","Unpredictable and unstable. Angina is an emergency because unstable angina precedes an MI.","Atypical Presentations","Women and people with diabetes","Symptoms are sometimes atypical: no classic radiation down the left arm.","Fatigue is a more prominent manifestation (a very big symptom); shortness of air; indigestion; anxiety.","Older patients may also present atypically.","A silent MI is one the person didn't know they had. Most MIs are not silent.","Angina Pain vs MI Pain","Angina pain","Precipitated by exertion or stress.","Relieved by rest and/or sublingual nitroglycerin.","Lasts less than 15 minutes.","MI pain","Occurs without a cause; sometimes wakes people from early-morning sleep (the instructor has read 4 to 6 a.m. is peak, possibly tied to the normal morning cortisol surge).","Relieved only by opioids (morphine).","Lasts 20 minutes or longer.","Often with nausea, diaphoresis, shortness of breath, and marked anxiety; can be associated with dysrhythmias.","Severe and immobilizing; not relieved by rest, position change, or nitrates.","Described as heaviness, pressure, tightness, burning, constriction, crushing (\"an elephant sitting on my chest\").","Ask about pressure, not just pain. A cardiac patient may say no to \"pain in your chest\" and yes to \"pressure in your chest.\" Choose words the patient understands.","How an MI Evolves","Time is muscle","Cells can stand about 20 minutes of ischemia before cell death starts.","The subendocardial layer is affected first; it takes about 4 to 6 hours for the entire thickness of the heart to necrose. (Chest pain at noon means full-thickness damage around 4 to 6 p.m.)","That is why ACS patients need to activate EMS and get to the hospital: waiting costs heart muscle.","The location of the MI correlates with the blocked coronary artery (an LAD blockage damages the apex, where the left ventricle is).","MIs are named by location: anterior, lateral, inferior, septal, posterior (for example, an inferior or inferolateral MI). You don't need to know this for the test; it is lingo you may hear.","Complications of an MI","Dysrhythmia — The most common complication of an MI and the most common cause of pre-hospital death (usually a ventricular dysrhythmia). Get a suspected MI on telemetry ASAP.","Heart failure — Muscle death, particularly at the apex and ventricle, loses the heart's pumping action. Keep MI and heart failure separate: they are different pathologies with different signs and symptoms, though one can impact the other.","Cardiogenic shock — Shock: loss of blood pressure and perfusion, and the body shuts down. Cardiogenic shock is when the heart is the problem, from severe left ventricular failure. High mortality.","Papillary muscle dysfunction — May produce a new murmur (listen to S1 and S2 for adventitious sounds).","Pericarditis — Occurs a couple of days after an MI, from the inflammatory response; shows up as a pericardial friction rub.","Diagnosing ACS: EKG Changes","The ST segment","We look for changes in the QRS, the ST segment, and the T wave (more in the dysrhythmia content).","At rest the ST segment should sit on the isoelectric line.","ST elevation = above the isoelectric line; with a positive troponin, that is a STEMI. Points to infarction (cell death, permanent).","ST depression = below the isoelectric line; sometimes happens with ischemia, sometimes infarction. Points to ischemia (temporary).","A STEMI shows ST elevation in two anatomically contiguous leads (contiguous = sharing a common border, touching each other).","A STEMI cannot be diagnosed by telemetry; it takes a 12-lead EKG.","Serial EKGs may be ordered because ischemia and infarction can change over a few hours.","STEMI vs NSTEMI","ST segment — Elevated — Depressed or normal","QRS — Often wide (\"wide and ugly\") — Normal","T wave — Peaked, then inverted — Inverted","Troponin — Elevated — Elevated","Occlusion — More extensive — More of an incomplete occlusion or transient thrombosis (shared with unstable angina)","Outcome — Larger infarct; people don't do as well — Better than a STEMI","Initial Care of the ACS Patient","Assess and stabilize","Consider ACS with chest discomfort, shortness of air, or other suggestive symptoms; remember atypical presentations in women, older patients, and patients with diabetes.","12-lead EKG within 10 minutes of arrival; repeat every 10 to 15 minutes if not definitive and MI is still suspected.","Airway, breathing, circulation. Sit the patient upright to support ventilation; supplemental oxygen per nasal cannula.","Baseline vital signs ASAP, including O2 saturation.","Telemetry ASAP to monitor for dysrhythmias (the number one complication) and ST changes. If you notice ST elevation on tele, the next best nursing action is a 12-lead EKG (cardiac nurses usually have a standing order).","IV access, for emergency or code medications.","Aspirin to chew and swallow (antiplatelet).","Assess pain with PQRST.","Pain Relief in ACS","Nitroglycerin first","0.4 mg sublingual is pretty standard, one at a time, spaced 5 minutes apart for persistent chest pain.","Check BP before each dose; it is likely to drop (a potent coronary vasodilator lowers peripheral vascular resistance).","If BP gets too low, switch to morphine.","Morphine","Eases the workload on the heart.","Decreases preload and afterload.","Helps with anxiety.","Patients often tolerate IV morphine better than nitroglycerin.","PQRST Pain Assessment","P — What started (precipitated) the pain?","Q — What does it feel like? Describe it for me.","R — Where is it, and does it radiate?","S — On a scale of 0 to 10, with 10 the worst imaginable, how bad is it right now?","T — When did it begin? (Timing helps decide the course of action.)","Labs and Ongoing Assessment","What to monitor","Cardiac markers: troponin is the most specific to cardiac muscle (some providers still order CK-MB or myoglobin).","Electrolytes, especially potassium, calcium, and magnesium: imbalances can be the root cause of dysrhythmias.","Hemoglobin and hematocrit; coagulation status (bleeding risk).","Heart sounds: listen for a murmur, gallop, or rub.","Lung sounds: an infarction in the left ventricle can lead to heart failure, and crackles could point to left-sided heart failure.","Treating Blockages: PCI, Thrombolytics & CABG","The three things the health care system can do for blockages found on a heart cath.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) — STEMI and NSTEMI. The first line of treatment for a confirmed MI; what the patient gets more times than not. — Within 90 minutes of ED arrival.","Thrombolytic (fibrinolytic) therapy — A STEMI patient may also be a candidate. — Within 30 minutes of ED arrival; most effective within 6 hours of the initial coronary event.","CABG (coronary artery bypass graft, \"open-heart surgery\") — Patients with diabetes, multi-vessel disease (3 or more vessels), or a blockage the catheter can't reach. — Emergent, or semi-elective (cath today, CABG the day after tomorrow).","PCI (Angioplasty, the \"Balloon Procedure\")","What happens","The heart cath shows whether blockages exist, where, how bad, and whether there is collateral circulation (little vessels the body grew around the blockage to perfuse the other side). It also evaluates left ventricular function.","It is a left heart cath (through the brachial or femoral artery) to reach the coronary arteries.","A guide wire and balloon catheter go to the occlusion; the balloon inflates and smashes the plaque back against the wall, then deflates and is removed.","Sometimes a stent is placed to reestablish patency.","Advantages over open-heart surgery","Percutaneous, through the vascular system; no cracking the chest.","Local anesthesia, not general.","Ambulatory shortly after.","Significantly shorter hospital stay; back to work and life sooner.","More PCIs are performed than CABGs.","PCI Nursing Care","What the CAD lecture adds for PCI","Monitor closely for MI progression: chest pain, EKG changes, new or different dysrhythmias, hemodynamic instability (unstable BP and heart rate).","Dual antiplatelet therapy: aspirin plus a drug like clopidogrel (Plavix). Possibly IV heparin during the procedure. It is a delicate balance; we don't want the patient to bleed out.","The sheath is usually removed 4 to 6 hours after the procedure; an arterial puncture needs manual pressure for a good 20 minutes.","Assess distal to the puncture: for a left groin site, compare the left foot's DP and PT pulses and cap refill to the right foot.","Watch for bleeding externally (the dressing) and internally: a heart rate creeping up can be the body compensating for lost blood volume.","Thrombolytic Therapy (\"Clot Busters\")","The drugs","Fibrinolytics: they lyse fibrin to dissolve thrombi in the coronary arteries and restore blood flow.","Examples: tPA and reteplase.","Per the lecture, given during the cardiac catheterization.","PCI is more commonly performed than thrombolytic therapy.","Monitor closely for bleeding after administration.","Who should NOT get them","History of a brain bleed or stroke, particularly hemorrhagic stroke.","Recent abdominal surgery (bleeding risk).","Active bleeding.","Coagulation studies that are out of range.","CABG (Coronary Artery Bypass Graft)","The procedure","The surgeon sews (anastomoses) a graft proximal and distal to the blockage, literally bypassing it. Up to six grafts (for example \"CABG times one,\" \"CABG times three\").","The patient's own vessels are used, such as the internal mammary artery or the saphenous vein (the long vein from the groin down the medial leg to the ankle). Veins have valves, so the graft is turned in favor of blood flow. Which vessel is…","Valve replacement is sometimes done at the same time.","Stroke and MI — Listed risks after open-heart surgery.","Infection — Sternal wound or the leg where the vein was harvested; poor healing is likelier with atherosclerosis or peripheral arterial/vascular disease (poor perfusion to the periphery) and can lengthen the stay.","Dysrhythmias — ICU for a while, then telemetry for the rest of the stay.","Pleural effusion — Fluid in the pleural cavity; third spacing.","Pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade — Excess fluid in the pericardial sac (pericardial effusion is to the heart as pleural effusion is to the lungs). A lot of fluid quickly squeezes the heart and it can't maintain cardiac output: car…","Renal failure — Monitor creatinine and urine output very closely.","Ongoing Care & Recovery","After the procedure","Continuous telemetry; rest, comfort, activity restriction for a while; good pain control.","Expect anxiety; teach at the level where the patient is, since everyone is in a different place. The nurse can be a game changer.","Timing matters for emotional and behavioral reactions. Example: resuming sexual activity, especially if that is what brought them in. Have those conversations before discharge so they understand their limitations.","Needs at home span physiological, psychological, mental, spiritual, economic, and vocational areas."]},{"s":"Part 3 — Pericarditis▾","a":"part-3-pericarditis","k":["Part 3 — Pericarditis▾","From Pericarditis. The first of three voiceovers on inflammatory, infective, and valvular heart disease. The friction-rub details from the CAD lecture (where pericarditis came up as an MI complication) are gathered here too, so the rub live…","The Pericardium","Layers and the potential space","The pericardium covers the heart. \"-itis\" means inflammation or infection: with pericarditis think inflammation (endocarditis is more about infection).","The fibrous pericardium is the outside; it circles back around into the visceral and parietal pericardium, with the pericardial space in between.","The pericardial space is a potential space holding usually 5 to 10 mL of fluid, just enough to keep the heart lubricated and smooth while it pumps.","Beneath are the myocardium and the endocardium.","Causes","Idiopathic — Often we really don't know why it happens.","Infective organisms — A variety sometimes precede pericarditis.","After an MI — A complication of an MI; usually shows up a few days after, from the MI's inflammation.","Connective tissue diseases — The pericardium is connective tissue. Examples: rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and lupus.","Pericarditis Pain","How it presents","Substernal / precordial (behind the breastbone), radiating to the left side of the neck, shoulder, and back.","A grating, oppressive feel (oppressive: hard to catch your breath).","Aggravated by breathing (mainly inspiration), coughing, and swallowing.","Worse lying down; better sitting up and leaning forward (leaning forward minimizes the stretch on the pericardium). A positional thing.","Pericarditis Pain vs MI Pain","After an MI you have to tell the two apart, since pericarditis is an MI complication.","Pericarditis pain","Onset usually more sudden.","Substernal, left precordial; may radiate.","Sharp and stabbing, or aching and dull; oppressive.","Worse on inspiration.","Body movement can increase it; leaning forward makes it better.","Can wax and wane.","Sublingual nitroglycerin won't help: nitro is a coronary vasodilator, and pericarditis has nothing to do with coronary vasodilation.","MI pain","Position is not going to matter.","Breathing isn't going to impact the pain.","Ischemia is what nitroglycerin addresses.","The Pericardial Friction Rub","The hallmark finding of pericarditis is the pericardial friction rub.","What it sounds like and why","A \"squeaky heart\"; a grating, continuous grinding sound, like two pieces of sandpaper rubbing together.","The visceral and parietal pericardium normally have a little fluid between them. Inflammation takes away that fluidity, and the two layers rub (CAD lecture).","Same idea as a pleural friction rub, when the visceral and parietal pleura are inflamed (for example, pleurisy).","How to listen","Stethoscope at the lower left sternal border.","Patient leaning forward (brings the heart closer to the chest wall).","Ask the patient to hold their breath to eliminate breath sounds and focus on the heart.","From the CAD lecture: if the grating disappears when the patient holds their breath, it is not cardiac in origin (it is respiratory).","Complications: Pericardial Effusion & Tamponade","Pericardial Effusion","Fluid in the pericardial space","Pericardial effusion is to the pericardium what pleural effusion is to the pleural space: third spacing, a collection of fluid that serves absolutely no purpose.","Symptoms come from the fluid squeezing the heart.","Cues: cough, tachypnea, difficulty breathing, and heart sounds that aren't crisp (fluid between the stethoscope and the heart).","The pericardial space doesn't expand much. As it fills, the heart gets squished, can't contract and release like it should, and cardiac output suffers.","Seen on an echocardiogram (or TEE), which delineates the heart's structures.","Cardiac Tamponade","Tamponade is time-sensitive and can be life-threatening. It develops when a pericardial effusion increases in volume.","Dyspnea, angina, restlessness, anxiety — Listed manifestations.","Decreased cardiac output (changes in vital signs) — Blood pressure is an indirect measure of cardiac output.","Marked jugular venous distension — Backup of fluid from the tamponade.","Very muffled heart sounds — Fluid around the heart.","Narrowed pulse pressure — See below.","Pulsus paradoxus (a classic) — See below.","Narrowed pulse pressure","Pulse pressure = systolic minus diastolic.","120/80 gives a pulse pressure of 40: pretty average.","In tamponade it narrows, such as 110/90: a pulse pressure of 20.","Pulsus paradoxus","BP normally drops a little with breathing, but not significantly. Significant = greater than 10 mmHg.","Systolic BP is 10 mmHg or more higher on expiration than on inspiration. (Lewis says it one way, UpToDate the other: 10 mmHg lower on inspiration. Same thing.)","For the skilled practitioner; the instructor has assessed it when the patient has an arterial line (systolic waxes and wanes with breathing). You won't get checked off on it.","1. Inflate the cuff beyond a palpable BP — You are palpating, not auscultating as in a normal BP.","2. Deflate gradually; note when sounds are first audible on expiration only — First number.","3. Note when sounds are audible on both inspiration and expiration — Second number.","4. A difference greater than 10 indicates tamponade — Lewis has a table, and there is a video on Canvas.","Diagnostics","EKG — Diffuse, widespread ST elevation in almost all leads. ST elevation in only two contiguous leads points to an acute MI instead.","Echocardiogram — Determines whether pericardial effusion or cardiac tamponade has occurred.","WBC, sed rate, CRP — May be elevated (it's an \"-itis\"). Sed rate and CRP are non-specific inflammation markers: sed rate more chronic, CRP more acute, and neither tells where the inflammation is.","Treatment","Bed rest, head of bed at 45 degrees — Plus measures to decrease anxiety.","Antibiotics — Only if bacteria is the causative agent; otherwise not indicated.","NSAIDs (the hallmark treatment) — Good old-fashioned NSAIDs: naproxen or high-dose ibuprofen (both over the counter). No morphine or opioids, because the problem is inflammation. Diagnosing it matters, because treatment differs from, say, p…","Colchicine — For recurrent pericarditis (covered again with arthritis and gout).","Prednisone taper — Only if NSAIDs are ineffective.","Pericardiocentesis (emergency) — Pericardiocentesis is to the pericardium as thoracentesis is to the pleural space: a needle removes the fluid. Per the lecture, removing about 5 to 10 mL can increase stroke volume by as much as 50%, letting…"]},{"s":"Part 4 — Infective Endocarditis▾","a":"part-4-infective-endocarditis","k":["Part 4 — Infective Endocarditis▾","From Infective Endocarditis. With pericarditis think inflammation; with endocarditis think infection. \"Infective endocarditis\" is the longer name, but it is usually just called endocarditis.","What It Is","The endocardium and the valves","The endocardium is the inside of the heart, which is a cue to where the problem is.","The valves are a very movable part of the heart, and a lot of the manifestations of endocarditis relate to the valves.","Endocarditis occurs when blood flow within the heart lets the organism infect previously damaged valves or another endothelial surface.","Subacute vs Acute","Subacute","Affects those with pre-existing heart problems.","Typically milder in presentation.","Can be more chronic.","Acute","Affects healthy valves.","Manifests itself quicker.","Therapy needs to be prompt and vigorous.","Who Gets It & What Causes It","Two things have to happen","A history of damaged epithelium, such as a valve replacement or a defect.","A port of entry for infection to reach the endocardium: a sore, a dental procedure, IV drug use.","Causative organisms","Most common: Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus viridans (bacterial).","Other possible pathogens: fungi and viruses, so not all endocarditis is bacterial.","Vegetations & Emboli","Why vegetations matter","Infection on the moving valves develops vegetations, for example mitral valve vegetation between the left atrium and left ventricle. Bacterial endocarditis of the mitral valve covers it with large, irregular vegetations.","The valves are constantly opening and closing, so vegetations can slough off and become emboli, dispersed into the vascular system.","Left-sided vegetation","Leaves the heart into the organs.","Can cause a limb infarction or an infarction in any organ of the body.","Right-sided vegetation","Blood from the right side goes to the lungs.","Like a pulmonary embolus.","Manifestations","Very non-specific at first","Timing: the transcript says it typically occurs \"within 10 two weeks\" of the infectious organism (unclear wording in the transcript, so no firm number is given here).","Low-grade fever, chills, malaise, fatigue, anorexia, joint pain, muscle pain. If you didn't know better, you'd think it was the flu.","Clubbing of the fingers is the odd one out, and it comes later in the disease process, not early.","Findings Unique to Endocarditis","The instructor says to commit these to memory: \"you don't hear about these in any other condition,\" and they \"would be great NCLEX questions.\"","Splinter hemorrhages — A longitudinal streak in the nail bed. — Vascular manifestation: vegetations leaving the left side of the heart and going to the periphery.","Petechiae — Little red hemorrhagic spots; smooth, flat (a macule), not raised, not painful. — Micro-emboli of vegetation. Fingers, eye, mouth, ankles, antecubital and popliteal areas.","Osler nodes — Tender papules on the fingertips and toes; look like they have a little pus in them. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive of (specific to) infective endocarditis.","Janeway lesions — Flat, macular, painless red spots on the palms and soles. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive.","Roth spots — Little hemorrhagic areas in the back of the retina. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive.","Telling the two apart: Osler nodes are tender (fingertips and toes); Janeway lesions are painless and flat (palms and soles).","Clinical Manifestations","New-onset murmur — A murmur is about the opening and closing of the valve. You should hear S1 and S2 crisply; damaged valves produce a murmur, an adventitious heart sound graded from 1 to 6.","Heart failure — The most common complication of infective endocarditis. Damaged valves leak blood back instead of letting it go one direction, the blood backs up, and the patient develops left- and right-sided heart failure.","History & Diagnostic Tests","History — Recent dental, urological, or gynecologic procedure; IV drug use.","Blood cultures — Three cultures drawn over an hour apart from three different sites; positive results are indicative of endocarditis.","Echocardiogram — Evidence of endocardial enlargement.","Prophylactic Antibiotics","Who gets prophylaxis","A mechanical heart valve.","A history of endocarditis.","A history of congenital heart disease.","A heart transplant.","Before which procedures","Any dental visit.","A biopsy of any kind.","Tonsil and adenoid removal.","A wound infection or urinary tract infection.","Treatment & Nursing Care","Treatment","Typically hospitalized initially; medications usually given IV.","Complete eradication may take weeks upon weeks; relapses are common.","Home care may include going home with a PICC line for IV antibiotics.","Nursing care and teaching","Identify and educate high-risk individuals so they can avoid infections.","Good rest; good oral hygiene (the mouth is where bacteria can enter).","Let the provider know before any dental procedure.","Refer IV drug users to drug rehab.","Monitor for the non-specific signs and symptoms that indicate a recurrence."]},{"s":"Part 5 — Valvular Heart Disease▾","a":"part-5-valvular-heart-disease","k":["Part 5 — Valvular Heart Disease▾","From Valvular Heart Disease. The last of the three inflammatory, infective, and valvular voiceovers. The instructor says you really need to know your valves: where they are and what they do.","Valve Review","What the valves do","Valves let blood leave one chamber and go into the next. When the next chamber contracts, the valve slams shut, keeping blood moving in a unidirectional path. Stenosis, prolapse, or regurgitation messes with that and causes backflow.","AV valves: tricuspid and mitral. Semilunar valves: pulmonic and aortic.","The mitral lies between the left atrium and left ventricle; the aortic lies after the left ventricle, before systemic circulation.","Left heart valves take more of a beating, because the left side is the high-pressure system. A repaired or replaced valve is, more times than not, the mitral or the aortic.","Auscultation Landmarks","Aortic — Right sternal border, 2nd ICS — S2 heard best (the base of the heart).","Pulmonic — Left sternal border, 2nd ICS","Erb's point — Left sternal border, 3rd ICS — S1 and S2 both heard best simultaneously in one place.","Tricuspid — 4th ICS","Mitral — 5th ICS, left midclavicular line — S1 heard best (the apex; closure of the mitral valve).","Types of Valve Problems","Stenosis — The door that won't open all the way. — Constriction or narrowing; blood backs up. — Often a history of rheumatic heart disease.","Regurgitation — The door that won't close all the way. — An incompetent valve; blood backs up into the previous chamber when it shouldn't. — Older age, or infective endocarditis.","Prolapse — The leaflets buckle. — Etiology unknown. The most common valve problem in the US; often seen in younger people and may improve with age.","Manifestations","They start to look like heart failure","Lewis has a long list; you don't need to memorize all of it. Stick with the classic ones below.","Regardless of the cause, blood backs up, and the patient shows left and right heart failure.","Backup from the right side goes to the periphery; backup from the left side shows up in the lungs.","Classic manifestations: exertional dyspnea, weakness, fatigue, possibly chest pain, maybe a murmur.","Conservative Management","Initially we try not to do surgery; treatment escalates if the patient becomes symptomatic enough.","Prophylactic antibiotics as necessary — To prevent rheumatic fever, rheumatic heart disease, and infective endocarditis.","Sodium restriction — \"Salt sucks and water follows salt\": keep excess fluid off board.","Heart failure medications — To treat and control the heart failure that has resulted from the valve problem.","Anticoagulants and/or antidysrhythmics — May be ordered.","Repairing the Valve","If surgery is needed, we would rather repair the patient's own valve than replace it. Percutaneous options include a commissurotomy, a transluminal balloon valvuloplasty, or a percutaneous valve replacement (such as an aortic valve replacem…","Commissurotomy — \"-otomy\" means opening: the provider opens up a stenosed valve.","Balloon valvuloplasty — For a stiff valve: a balloon is inflated to open it up. Like a commissurotomy, the goal is to open a valve that isn't as open as it should be, without replacing it.","Annuloplasty — Surgery to repair and reshape the valve, using annuloplasty rings so it works better.","Replacing the Valve: Mechanical vs Biologic","Mechanical valve","More durable; lasts longer.","Needs lifetime anticoagulation: the body sees the mechanical pieces as foreign, and clots might form. That is the disadvantage.","For someone who will be compliant with lifetime anticoagulation.","You will hear a click (it is metal). Some say you can hear it from the bedside without a stethoscope.","Biologic valve (usually porcine, a pig valve)","Less durable; doesn't last as long (less than 8 to 10 years).","No need for anticoagulation.","For someone older with a shorter life expectancy, or someone unwilling or unable to adhere to anticoagulation.","No click; you hear only the closure of the valve.","Adventitious heart sounds so far: a rub, a murmur, a gallop, and now a click. Who has a click? Someone with a mechanical valve.","After Valve Replacement","Recovery and anticoagulation","Recovery is similar to a CABG; sometimes valves are replaced at the same time as the bypass (\"killing two birds with one stone\").","A prosthetic valve means anticoagulation. On warfarin (Coumadin), the INR is monitored for a lifetime.","Per the lecture: normal INR 0.8 to 1.1; goal INR on warfarin 2.5 to 3.5. Thin the blood, but not too thin; any higher and they could have issues.","Antidote for bleeding: vitamin K.","Eat vitamin K foods consistently (kale greens, for example); don't binge — Vitamin K messes with the clotting factors and reverses the effect of warfarin.","Electric razor instead of a straight-edge razor — Take measures not to bleed.","Monitor for any signs of excessive bleeding — Anticoagulated.","Avoid an MRI — The valve has metal.","Wear a medical alert bracelet or card — Always."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Mixed across all five Week 1 topics, built around the look-alikes that are easy to mix up. Click a card to flip it."]}]},{"f":"week1-cv-labs-diagnostics.html","t":"Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics","secs":[{"s":"Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 2","Serum Cardiac Markers","Inflammation Markers: CRP & ESR","BNP & Pro-BNP","Cholesterol & Lipoproteins","Non-Invasive Procedures","Invasive Procedures","Self-Test Flashcards","Built from the two Week 1 labs-and-diagnostics voiceovers: serum labs and non-invasive tests (part 1), then the invasive procedures (part 2). The instructor says you will not need to memorize the numeric ranges for most of these labs; the e…"]},{"s":"Serum Cardiac Markers▾","a":"serum-cardiac-markers","k":["Serum Cardiac Markers▾","When a patient has a myocardial infarction (MI), there is heart cell death, and the markers below are what the blood draw shows.","Troponin — The most sensitive indicator of myocardial damage. Helps differentiate cardiac from non-cardiac chest pain (for example, cardiac versus GI substernal pressure). — Elevates 2 to 3 hours after the MI; returns to baseline in 10 to 1…","CK-MB — Muscle damage, but not specific to the heart. — Elevated 3 hours after an MI; stays elevated 24 hours to 2 days. — Historically important and used before troponin existed; \"NCLEX still likes to talk about it.\" Not as specific as tro…","Myoglobin — Another non-specific marker of myocardial damage (less specific than troponin). — Not given. — Mentioned only because it shows up on some standardized tests. The instructor says you don't need to know myoglobin."]},{"s":"Inflammation Markers: CRP & ESR▾","a":"inflammation-markers-crp-esr","k":["Inflammation Markers: CRP & ESR▾","CRP (C-reactive protein)","Indicates inflammation in the body; non-specific (it doesn't say where).","More sensitive than the ESR.","Peaks within 18 to 72 hours after an MI; failure to normalize might indicate ongoing damage to the heart muscle.","With an MI there is inflammation in addition to heart cell death.","Monitors more closely for the acute picture.","ESR (sed rate)","Also a non-specific indicator of inflammation; could possibly be infection.","More useful for monitoring chronic inflammation.","CRP and ESR often go hand in hand.","Neither tells you where in the body the inflammation is (it could be an arthritis flare, for instance)."]},{"s":"BNP & Pro-BNP▾","a":"bnp-pro-bnp","k":["BNP & Pro-BNP▾","Brain natriuretic peptide","Despite the word \"brain,\" the main source of BNP is the ventricles of the heart.","Helps distinguish cardiac versus respiratory causes of dyspnea: a short-of-breath patient in the ED, where the provider wants to know if it is heart failure or respiratory failure.","An elevated BNP indicates left ventricular dysfunction.","Pro-BNP is what the instructor sees most often at UK. Plasma concentrations of BNP and pro-BNP are similar, but with left ventricular dysfunction (left-sided heart failure) pro-BNP rises much more significantly than BNP.","No ranges given: you don't need to memorize numbers for this, and NCLEX won't expect you to.","Rule of thumb from the lecture: BNP or pro-BNP elevated, think heart failure. Troponin elevated, think myocardial infarction."]},{"s":"Cholesterol & Lipoproteins▾","a":"cholesterol-lipoproteins","k":["Cholesterol & Lipoproteins▾","Total cholesterol","The main lipid associated with cardiovascular disease.","We eat it and we also manufacture it; high cholesterol can be inherited (familial).","Done as part of a lipid profile; not a totally accurate predictor of heart disease by itself.","Diurnal variation: a morning reading can differ from an afternoon reading.","Affected by malnutrition: if it is very low, also look at the serum albumin to see how malnourished the patient is.","HDL and LDL","An accurate predictor of heart disease.","HDL = the \"good\" cholesterol; removes cholesterol in the body.","LDL = the \"bad\" cholesterol; deposits cholesterol in the peripheral tissue.","We like high HDLs and low LDLs.","The values help determine whether the patient is low, moderate, or high risk for CAD.","The one number to know: total cholesterol less than 200. The instructor does not ask you to memorize the LDL, HDL, or triglyceride numbers; a test question would speak in general terms."]},{"s":"Non-Invasive Procedures▾","a":"non-invasive-procedures","k":["Non-Invasive Procedures▾","12-lead EKG (ECG) — Looks at the heart's conduction system from 12 different perspectives simultaneously. A snapshot in time (takes 2 to 3 minutes at the bedside). — The provider can diagnose an MI with a 12-lead. Nurses and nursing student…","Telemetry (\"tele\") — Ongoing, continuous monitoring of the cardiac rhythm (telebox around the neck, chest electrodes, or bedside monitoring; may be watched at a central monitoring area). — Only a one-way perspective of the heart's rhythm. I…","Ambulatory EKG (Holter monitor) — Like a 24-hour EKG, worn at home to capture heart activity, for example with syncope of unknown origin that could be a dysrhythmia. — The patient keeps a diary (exercise, anything that feels funny in the he…","Stress test — Evaluates the heart's response to exercise (treadmill or exercise bike), stressing the heart on purpose. — For chest pain with exertion but not at rest. CAD may show minimal symptoms and a normal resting EKG; exercise unmasks …","Echocardiogram (\"echo\") — Uses sound waves to create a moving picture of the heart. — Evaluates the valves, chamber size, and time in diastole and systole; measures ejection fraction. No radiation, no prep, painless. Bedside or outpatient; …","12-Lead EKG vs Telemetry","12-lead EKG","12 perspectives at once.","A snapshot in time.","Can diagnose an MI.","Telemetry","A one-way perspective.","Continuous and ongoing.","Monitors only; cannot diagnose.","Ejection Fraction","Definition and values","The amount of blood pumped out of the left ventricle with each contraction; an indication of how effectively the heart is pumping.","Measured with an echocardiogram, and (per the lecture) also with a right-sided heart cath.","Normal: 55% to 70% of the blood sitting in the ventricle at the end of diastole is ejected (about half or a little more).","Low values, such as 40%, are seen in heart failure. A really, really low ejection fraction can mean the patient might need a heart transplant.","Very important information for a patient who might have heart failure."]},{"s":"Invasive Procedures▾","a":"invasive-procedures","k":["Invasive Procedures▾","TEE (transesophageal echocardiogram) — A probe is inserted into the esophagus. The plain echo shows the anterior heart; the TEE shows the posterior heart and can give a lot more information. Done in the vascular lab. — Contrast dye may be u…","EP study (electrophysiology study) — Evaluates the electrical health of the heart, usually for dysrhythmias not responding to standard treatment. Dysrhythmias are induced on purpose in a controlled environment to confirm the root of the ele…","Cardiac catheterization with angiography (\"heart cath\"; some older patients call it a \"dye test\") — Dye plus pictures. Gives information about the heart muscle, the coronary arteries, and the valves. Diagnostic: confirms the location and ex…","Left vs Right Heart Cath","Left heart cath","Enter an artery (femoral, radial, or brachial).","The catheter goes retrograde up into the ascending aorta / arch of the aorta.","The goal is always to get to the coronary arteries; this is the absolute only way to reach them.","Used when a patient has chest pain or angina and the question is coronary occlusion.","Places examined: the aorta, right coronary artery, left anterior descending, circumflex, and left main coronary artery; the left ventricle sits at the apex.","Right heart cath","Enter the venous system.","Can measure ejection fraction and look at the pressures of the right side of the heart.","Cannot look at the coronary arteries.","Sometimes only one side is done; a lot of times both are done.","Before the Heart Cath","Pre-procedure","NPO; meds with sips of water may be allowed (provider decision).","Signed consent (definitely invasive); the patient and family need to understand the procedure and what to expect.","Very little pain, because blood vessels don't have nerve fibers.","Warn the patient about the warm flush / hot flash when the contrast dye is injected; it is an expected finding, so they don't think something is wrong.","Pre-procedure checklist; monitor BUN and creatinine (dye can be nephrotoxic); empty the bladder before going.","Local anesthesia and likely IV sedation to keep them from being anxious.","After the Heart Cath","Recovery in the cath lab observation area before returning to the room — Standard recovery location.","Monitor clotting time closely before the sheath is pulled — Anticoagulants are used during the procedure and DC'd afterward; arteries are high-pressure vessels, so bleeding is possible (especially after a heparin drip).","Sheath removed, then pressure held up to about 20 minutes — A long time compared to a regular IV, because the vessel needs to seal.","Bed rest at least 8 hours if the femoral (groin) site was used; affected leg straight; head of bed below 30 degrees — So the vessel doesn't bleed.","Frequent assessment of circulation distal to the puncture: pulse, color, sensation — Make sure there is good perfusion on the other side of where the vessel was penetrated.","Potential Complications of a Heart Cath","Bleeding (number one) — An IV heparin drip is used during the procedure to decrease clot risk; monitor the puncture site closely for hematomas.","Emboli — Monitor for signs and symptoms of stroke.","Infection — A listed risk.","Dysrhythmias — A listed risk.","Damage to the heart — If the pericardium is accidentally poked, the patient may develop cardiac tamponade.","Allergy to the dye — Monitor for it.","Contrast nephrotoxicity — Risk is higher in kidney failure; see below.","Cardiac tamponade is named here as a cath complication — review it in Pericarditis","Contrast Nephrotoxicity","Acute kidney injury after dye","An acute kidney injury (AKI) that happens after a dye injection; a major cause of AKI in the hospitalized patient.","Increased risk with existing renal problems, diabetes, or fluid volume deficit.","Acetylcysteine, and sometimes sodium bicarbonate, are given before the procedure to offset the elevated BUN and creatinine.","Encourage the patient to force fluids afterward to dilute the dye going through the kidneys."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-cad-acs.html","t":"CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome","secs":[{"s":"CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 3","Coronary Arteries & Terminology","The CAD Continuum","Risk Factors for CAD","Chronic Stable Angina","Nitroglycerin","Acute Coronary Syndrome: Unstable Angina, NSTEMI & STEMI","Angina Pain vs MI Pain","How an MI Evolves","Complications of an MI","Diagnosing ACS: EKG Changes","Initial Care of the ACS Patient","Treating Blockages: PCI, Thrombolytics & CABG","Ongoing Care & Recovery","Self-Test Flashcards","Built from the three Week 1 coronary voiceovers: coronary heart disease and chronic stable angina (part 1), acute coronary syndrome and MI (part 2), and caring for the patient with ACS, including PCI, thrombolytics, and CABG (part 3)."]},{"s":"Coronary Arteries & Terminology▾","a":"coronary-arteries-terminology","k":["Coronary Arteries & Terminology▾","The basics","Coronary heart disease, coronary artery disease (CAD), and ischemic heart disease are used interchangeably.","The coronary arteries perfuse the heart, getting oxygen to the heart muscle. We want patent coronary arteries.","You can predict what area of the heart an artery perfuses from the anatomy. The instructor is not going to ask you to memorize the arteries, but the providers look at a few main ones: the left main, the left anterior descending (LAD), the c…","The left main and LAD perfuse the left ventricle, the powerhouse chamber. Complete blockage there is sometimes called the \"widow maker\", because those patients often never make it to the hospital.","Atherosclerosis (\"hardening of the arteries\") is responsible for almost all cases of CAD, regardless of type."]},{"s":"The CAD Continuum▾","a":"the-cad-continuum","k":["The CAD Continuum▾","Think of coronary artery disease as a continuum, from chronic ischemic heart disease to acute coronary syndrome (ACS). Once it starts looking like unstable angina, it is collectively called ACS, because time is of the essence if it advances…","Stable angina — Unstable angina — NSTEMI — STEMI","Chronic ischemic heart disease; the patient lives with it. — ACS. No heart damage. — ACS. Heart damage. — ACS. More extensive heart damage.","\"A STEMI is worse than a non-STEMI, a non-STEMI is worse than unstable angina, and unstable angina is worse than stable angina.\""]},{"s":"Risk Factors for CAD▾","a":"risk-factors-for-cad","k":["Risk Factors for CAD▾","Non-modifiable","Age: the older someone gets, the more likely CAD is.","Gender: males are at greater risk than females.","Family history: especially relatives who developed CAD younger.","The four most firmly established modifiable","Elevated serum lipids (cholesterol, triglycerides); we want high HDLs and low LDLs.","Hypertension: untreated, the increased arterial pressure causes damage over time (the hardening). It can be silent and asymptomatic, so people don't perceive it as a big deal. Patients with CKD or diabetes have an even lower BP goal.","Tobacco use: risk is proportional to the number of cigarettes smoked.","Physical inactivity.","Other modifiable risk factors","Obesity: apple-shaped people (rotund in the belly) are at higher risk than pear-shaped people (hips wider than the abdomen).","Diabetes mellitus: poor glucose control aggravates coronary heart disease.","Depression or prolonged stress.","Teaching for Each Modifiable Risk Factor","Elevated serum lipids — Monitor lipids regularly; diet control (low-fat diet); statin medications.","Hypertension — Check BP regularly; antihypertensive medications; DASH diet (low-salt, low-fat).","Tobacco — Don't start; if they smoke, encourage stopping. Switching to lower-nicotine or filtered cigarettes does not impact the risk. The benefits of cessation are dramatic and start almost immediately, even after 20 years of smoking.","Physical inactivity — Middle-aged patients or those with a history of medical problems should see the provider before starting. Exercise sessions at least 40 minutes long with a 10-minute warm-up and cool-down, moderately 3 to 4 times a wee…","Obesity — Avoid fad diets; restrict saturated fats, sweets, and high-cholesterol foods; increase physical activity.","Diabetes — Tight glucose control: glycemic index diet, carb counting, diabetes education, hemoglobin A1C checked regularly.","Stress — Incorporate stress-reduction activities."]},{"s":"Chronic Stable Angina▾","a":"chronic-stable-angina","k":["Chronic Stable Angina▾","Angina means chest pain from a temporary loss of oxygenation to the heart muscle, more often than not from an occluded coronary artery (but you can't know without the tests).","Where the pain can be","Substernal, possibly radiating up to the neck.","The classic: substernal, radiating down the left arm. It can radiate down both arms.","Epigastric (troponins help rule cardiac in or out; sometimes it is just gastric reflux).","Mid-scapular. The instructor once had a patient with pain in the elbows: it is not exactly predictable.","What makes it \"stable\"","Occurs with moderate to prolonged exertion, in a pattern familiar to the patient.","Frequency, duration, and intensity stay pretty much stable.","Only slight limitations in activity; regulated on medication.","Usually associated with fixed atherosclerotic plaque.","Relieved by rest and/or nitroglycerin.","Managed with drug therapy; rarely requires aggressive therapy.","Diagnosing Stable Angina","Workup","Physical exam and complete history; EKG.","Exercise stress test, or a pharmacological stress test if they can't use the treadmill or bike: a drug with positive inotropic and chronotropic effects (increased contractility and heart rate) to stress the heart on purpose.","Low and intermediate risk results: manage symptoms without an intervention.","High risk results: more testing, such as cardiac catheterization followed by revascularization (PCI or CABG), and/or an echo or TEE.","Stress test, echo, and TEE come up again here — review them in CV Labs & Diagnostics","Goals and Medications for Stable Angina","Goal: decrease the workload on the heart and increase the oxygen supply, targeting the modifiable risk factors as appropriate.","Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid), antiplatelet — 81 mg (the chewable) or 325 mg (the adult form); evidence doesn't show one works better. Anyone at risk for CAD needs to be taking an aspirin a day.","Lipid-lowering agents — Already covered in patho.","Beta blockers (\"-olol\") — The only classification proven to prevent re-infarction and improve survival for MI patients. Long-acting beta blockers are sometimes used.","Calcium channel blockers — Usually given in conjunction with the beta blocker.","Nitrates — Typically sublingual; first-line treatment for acute anginal symptoms. Long-acting nitrates also exist.","Sodium current inhibitors (Ranexa) — The newer therapy."]},{"s":"Nitroglycerin▾","a":"nitroglycerin","k":["Nitroglycerin▾","Current guideline: if chest pain has not improved, or has worsened, 5 minutes after the first nitro, the patient calls 911 and chews an aspirin immediately (antiplatelet effects begin within an hour and continue for several days). The old t…","Sublingual (most common) — Rapid acting. Under the tongue; don't swallow. Repeat every 5 minutes times 3 if needed. At home, if one doesn't work, activate EMS. In the hospital, take vital signs before each dose; you might give it every 5 mi…","Translingual — Rapid acting; on the tongue.","IV — Rapid acting; dosed in micrograms per minute (med math). Comes in a glass bottle with special vented tubing.","Skin patch and ointment — Use gloves to apply and remove (it can vasodilate you and give you a horrendous headache). Never defibrillate over the patch.","Isosorbide — For preventing angina attacks; taken daily. Tolerance builds up over time.","Teaching about sublingual nitro","Take it at the beginning of chest pain, or prophylactically before activity known to cause it (walking, bike riding, sexual activity).","Works in less than 3 minutes; keep it accessible (in your pocket).","Protect it from light (brown bottle); replace the bottle every 6 months.","It should tingle as it dissolves; no tingle may mean it has expired. Continued pain or no tingle: activate EMS.","Never combine with erectile dysfunction drugs; this can be fatal.","Side effects (all from vasodilation)","Headache (not a reason to skip the nitro).","Lowered BP: fainting and postural hypotension.","Flushing.","Always know the blood pressure before giving nitroglycerin, because it is going to lower it. A patient with a low baseline (for example 90/60) can plummet. If the BP is too low, no more nitro is given; something different, like IV morphine,…"]},{"s":"Acute Coronary Syndrome: Unstable Angina, NSTEMI & STEMI▾","a":"acute-coronary-syndrome-unstable-angina-nstemi-stemi","k":["Acute Coronary Syndrome: Unstable Angina, NSTEMI & STEMI▾","ACS is what happens when the slippery slope begins and the patient has more signs and symptoms than we want. It is divided into unstable angina and acute MI.","Unstable angina — No changes (or ST depression / deep T-wave inversion) — Not elevated — None, but a sign to be vigilant about slipping into an NSTEMI or STEMI","NSTEMI (non-ST-elevation MI) — No ST elevation (ST depression / deep T-wave inversion, or no changes) — Elevated — Yes","STEMI (ST-elevation MI) — ST elevation — Elevated — Yes, more extensive","Troponin timing (up in 2 to 3 hours, baseline in 10 to 14 days) is repeated here — review it in CV Labs & Diagnostics","Unstable Angina","How it differs from stable angina","Often occurs at rest, unannounced and unprecipitated; usually lasts more than 20 minutes.","New onset that markedly limits physical activity (stops the person in their tracks).","Increasing angina: more frequent, longer, with less exertion than the patient's previous stable angina.","Poorly relieved by rest or nitroglycerin.","Unpredictable and unstable. Angina is an emergency because unstable angina precedes an MI.","Atypical Presentations","Women and people with diabetes","Symptoms are sometimes atypical: no classic radiation down the left arm.","Fatigue is a more prominent manifestation (a very big symptom); shortness of air; indigestion; anxiety.","Older patients may also present atypically.","A silent MI is one the person didn't know they had. Most MIs are not silent."]},{"s":"Angina Pain vs MI Pain▾","a":"angina-pain-vs-mi-pain","k":["Angina Pain vs MI Pain▾","Angina pain","Precipitated by exertion or stress.","Relieved by rest and/or sublingual nitroglycerin.","Lasts less than 15 minutes.","MI pain","Occurs without a cause; sometimes wakes people from early-morning sleep (the instructor has read 4 to 6 a.m. is peak, possibly tied to the normal morning cortisol surge).","Relieved only by opioids (morphine).","Lasts 20 minutes or longer.","Often with nausea, diaphoresis, shortness of breath, and marked anxiety; can be associated with dysrhythmias.","Severe and immobilizing; not relieved by rest, position change, or nitrates.","Described as heaviness, pressure, tightness, burning, constriction, crushing (\"an elephant sitting on my chest\").","Ask about pressure, not just pain. A cardiac patient may say no to \"pain in your chest\" and yes to \"pressure in your chest.\" Choose words the patient understands."]},{"s":"How an MI Evolves▾","a":"how-an-mi-evolves","k":["How an MI Evolves▾","Time is muscle","Cells can stand about 20 minutes of ischemia before cell death starts.","The subendocardial layer is affected first; it takes about 4 to 6 hours for the entire thickness of the heart to necrose. (Chest pain at noon means full-thickness damage around 4 to 6 p.m.)","That is why ACS patients need to activate EMS and get to the hospital: waiting costs heart muscle.","The location of the MI correlates with the blocked coronary artery (an LAD blockage damages the apex, where the left ventricle is).","MIs are named by location: anterior, lateral, inferior, septal, posterior (for example, an inferior or inferolateral MI). You don't need to know this for the test; it is lingo you may hear."]},{"s":"Complications of an MI▾","a":"complications-of-an-mi","k":["Complications of an MI▾","Dysrhythmia — The most common complication of an MI and the most common cause of pre-hospital death (usually a ventricular dysrhythmia). Get a suspected MI on telemetry ASAP.","Heart failure — Muscle death, particularly at the apex and ventricle, loses the heart's pumping action. Keep MI and heart failure separate: they are different pathologies with different signs and symptoms, though one can impact the other.","Cardiogenic shock — Shock: loss of blood pressure and perfusion, and the body shuts down. Cardiogenic shock is when the heart is the problem, from severe left ventricular failure. High mortality.","Papillary muscle dysfunction — May produce a new murmur (listen to S1 and S2 for adventitious sounds).","Pericarditis — Occurs a couple of days after an MI, from the inflammatory response; shows up as a pericardial friction rub.","Pericarditis and the friction rub are covered again here — review them in Pericarditis"]},{"s":"Diagnosing ACS: EKG Changes▾","a":"diagnosing-acs-ekg-changes","k":["Diagnosing ACS: EKG Changes▾","The ST segment","We look for changes in the QRS, the ST segment, and the T wave (more in the dysrhythmia content).","At rest the ST segment should sit on the isoelectric line.","ST elevation = above the isoelectric line; with a positive troponin, that is a STEMI. Points to infarction (cell death, permanent).","ST depression = below the isoelectric line; sometimes happens with ischemia, sometimes infarction. Points to ischemia (temporary).","A STEMI shows ST elevation in two anatomically contiguous leads (contiguous = sharing a common border, touching each other).","A STEMI cannot be diagnosed by telemetry; it takes a 12-lead EKG.","Serial EKGs may be ordered because ischemia and infarction can change over a few hours.","STEMI vs NSTEMI","ST segment — Elevated — Depressed or normal","QRS — Often wide (\"wide and ugly\") — Normal","T wave — Peaked, then inverted — Inverted","Troponin — Elevated — Elevated","Occlusion — More extensive — More of an incomplete occlusion or transient thrombosis (shared with unstable angina)","Outcome — Larger infarct; people don't do as well — Better than a STEMI"]},{"s":"Initial Care of the ACS Patient▾","a":"initial-care-of-the-acs-patient","k":["Initial Care of the ACS Patient▾","Assess and stabilize","Consider ACS with chest discomfort, shortness of air, or other suggestive symptoms; remember atypical presentations in women, older patients, and patients with diabetes.","12-lead EKG within 10 minutes of arrival; repeat every 10 to 15 minutes if not definitive and MI is still suspected.","Airway, breathing, circulation. Sit the patient upright to support ventilation; supplemental oxygen per nasal cannula.","Baseline vital signs ASAP, including O2 saturation.","Telemetry ASAP to monitor for dysrhythmias (the number one complication) and ST changes. If you notice ST elevation on tele, the next best nursing action is a 12-lead EKG (cardiac nurses usually have a standing order).","IV access, for emergency or code medications.","Aspirin to chew and swallow (antiplatelet).","Assess pain with PQRST.","Pain Relief in ACS","Nitroglycerin first","0.4 mg sublingual is pretty standard, one at a time, spaced 5 minutes apart for persistent chest pain.","Check BP before each dose; it is likely to drop (a potent coronary vasodilator lowers peripheral vascular resistance).","If BP gets too low, switch to morphine.","Morphine","Eases the workload on the heart.","Decreases preload and afterload.","Helps with anxiety.","Patients often tolerate IV morphine better than nitroglycerin.","PQRST Pain Assessment","P — What started (precipitated) the pain?","Q — What does it feel like? Describe it for me.","R — Where is it, and does it radiate?","S — On a scale of 0 to 10, with 10 the worst imaginable, how bad is it right now?","T — When did it begin? (Timing helps decide the course of action.)","Labs and Ongoing Assessment","What to monitor","Cardiac markers: troponin is the most specific to cardiac muscle (some providers still order CK-MB or myoglobin).","Electrolytes, especially potassium, calcium, and magnesium: imbalances can be the root cause of dysrhythmias.","Hemoglobin and hematocrit; coagulation status (bleeding risk).","Heart sounds: listen for a murmur, gallop, or rub.","Lung sounds: an infarction in the left ventricle can lead to heart failure, and crackles could point to left-sided heart failure."]},{"s":"Treating Blockages: PCI, Thrombolytics & CABG▾","a":"treating-blockages-pci-thrombolytics-cabg","k":["Treating Blockages: PCI, Thrombolytics & CABG▾","The three things the health care system can do for blockages found on a heart cath.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) — STEMI and NSTEMI. The first line of treatment for a confirmed MI; what the patient gets more times than not. — Within 90 minutes of ED arrival.","Thrombolytic (fibrinolytic) therapy — A STEMI patient may also be a candidate. — Within 30 minutes of ED arrival; most effective within 6 hours of the initial coronary event.","CABG (coronary artery bypass graft, \"open-heart surgery\") — Patients with diabetes, multi-vessel disease (3 or more vessels), or a blockage the catheter can't reach. — Emergent, or semi-elective (cath today, CABG the day after tomorrow).","PCI (Angioplasty, the \"Balloon Procedure\")","What happens","The heart cath shows whether blockages exist, where, how bad, and whether there is collateral circulation (little vessels the body grew around the blockage to perfuse the other side). It also evaluates left ventricular function.","It is a left heart cath (through the brachial or femoral artery) to reach the coronary arteries.","A guide wire and balloon catheter go to the occlusion; the balloon inflates and smashes the plaque back against the wall, then deflates and is removed.","Sometimes a stent is placed to reestablish patency.","Advantages over open-heart surgery","Percutaneous, through the vascular system; no cracking the chest.","Local anesthesia, not general.","Ambulatory shortly after.","Significantly shorter hospital stay; back to work and life sooner.","More PCIs are performed than CABGs.","PCI Nursing Care","Recovery is very similar to a heart cath — review post-cath care in CV Labs & Diagnostics","What the CAD lecture adds for PCI","Monitor closely for MI progression: chest pain, EKG changes, new or different dysrhythmias, hemodynamic instability (unstable BP and heart rate).","Dual antiplatelet therapy: aspirin plus a drug like clopidogrel (Plavix). Possibly IV heparin during the procedure. It is a delicate balance; we don't want the patient to bleed out.","The sheath is usually removed 4 to 6 hours after the procedure; an arterial puncture needs manual pressure for a good 20 minutes.","Assess distal to the puncture: for a left groin site, compare the left foot's DP and PT pulses and cap refill to the right foot.","Watch for bleeding externally (the dressing) and internally: a heart rate creeping up can be the body compensating for lost blood volume.","Thrombolytic Therapy (\"Clot Busters\")","The drugs","Fibrinolytics: they lyse fibrin to dissolve thrombi in the coronary arteries and restore blood flow.","Examples: tPA and reteplase.","Per the lecture, given during the cardiac catheterization.","PCI is more commonly performed than thrombolytic therapy.","Monitor closely for bleeding after administration.","Who should NOT get them","History of a brain bleed or stroke, particularly hemorrhagic stroke.","Recent abdominal surgery (bleeding risk).","Active bleeding.","Coagulation studies that are out of range.","CABG (Coronary Artery Bypass Graft)","The procedure","The surgeon sews (anastomoses) a graft proximal and distal to the blockage, literally bypassing it. Up to six grafts (for example \"CABG times one,\" \"CABG times three\").","The patient's own vessels are used, such as the internal mammary artery or the saphenous vein (the long vein from the groin down the medial leg to the ankle). Veins have valves, so the graft is turned in favor of blood flow. Which vessel is…","Valve replacement is sometimes done at the same time.","Stroke and MI — Listed risks after open-heart surgery.","Infection — Sternal wound or the leg where the vein was harvested; poor healing is likelier with atherosclerosis or peripheral arterial/vascular disease (poor perfusion to the periphery) and can lengthen the stay.","Dysrhythmias — ICU for a while, then telemetry for the rest of the stay.","Pleural effusion — Fluid in the pleural cavity; third spacing.","Pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade — Excess fluid in the pericardial sac (pericardial effusion is to the heart as pleural effusion is to the lungs). A lot of fluid quickly squeezes the heart and it can't maintain cardiac output: car…","Renal failure — Monitor creatinine and urine output very closely.","Pericardial effusion and tamponade come up again here — review them in Pericarditis"]},{"s":"Ongoing Care & Recovery▾","a":"ongoing-care-recovery","k":["Ongoing Care & Recovery▾","After the procedure","Continuous telemetry; rest, comfort, activity restriction for a while; good pain control.","Expect anxiety; teach at the level where the patient is, since everyone is in a different place. The nurse can be a game changer.","Timing matters for emotional and behavioral reactions. Example: resuming sexual activity, especially if that is what brought them in. Have those conversations before discharge so they understand their limitations.","Needs at home span physiological, psychological, mental, spiritual, economic, and vocational areas."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-pericarditis.html","t":"Pericarditis","secs":[{"s":"Pericarditis","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 4","The Pericardium","Causes","Pericarditis Pain","The Pericardial Friction Rub","Complications: Pericardial Effusion & Tamponade","Diagnostics","Treatment","Self-Test Flashcards","The first of three voiceovers on inflammatory, infective, and valvular heart disease. The friction-rub details from the CAD lecture (where pericarditis came up as an MI complication) are gathered here too, so the rub lives in one place."]},{"s":"The Pericardium▾","a":"the-pericardium","k":["The Pericardium▾","Layers and the potential space","The pericardium covers the heart. \"-itis\" means inflammation or infection: with pericarditis think inflammation (endocarditis is more about infection).","The fibrous pericardium is the outside; it circles back around into the visceral and parietal pericardium, with the pericardial space in between.","The pericardial space is a potential space holding usually 5 to 10 mL of fluid, just enough to keep the heart lubricated and smooth while it pumps.","Beneath are the myocardium and the endocardium."]},{"s":"Causes▾","a":"causes","k":["Causes▾","Idiopathic — Often we really don't know why it happens.","Infective organisms — A variety sometimes precede pericarditis.","After an MI — A complication of an MI; usually shows up a few days after, from the MI's inflammation.","Connective tissue diseases — The pericardium is connective tissue. Examples: rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and lupus."]},{"s":"Pericarditis Pain▾","a":"pericarditis-pain","k":["Pericarditis Pain▾","How it presents","Substernal / precordial (behind the breastbone), radiating to the left side of the neck, shoulder, and back.","A grating, oppressive feel (oppressive: hard to catch your breath).","Aggravated by breathing (mainly inspiration), coughing, and swallowing.","Worse lying down; better sitting up and leaning forward (leaning forward minimizes the stretch on the pericardium). A positional thing.","Pericarditis Pain vs MI Pain","After an MI you have to tell the two apart, since pericarditis is an MI complication.","Pericarditis pain","Onset usually more sudden.","Substernal, left precordial; may radiate.","Sharp and stabbing, or aching and dull; oppressive.","Worse on inspiration.","Body movement can increase it; leaning forward makes it better.","Can wax and wane.","Sublingual nitroglycerin won't help: nitro is a coronary vasodilator, and pericarditis has nothing to do with coronary vasodilation.","MI pain","Position is not going to matter.","Breathing isn't going to impact the pain.","Ischemia is what nitroglycerin addresses.","MI pain is described in full in CAD & ACS"]},{"s":"The Pericardial Friction Rub▾","a":"the-pericardial-friction-rub","k":["The Pericardial Friction Rub▾","The hallmark finding of pericarditis is the pericardial friction rub.","What it sounds like and why","A \"squeaky heart\"; a grating, continuous grinding sound, like two pieces of sandpaper rubbing together.","The visceral and parietal pericardium normally have a little fluid between them. Inflammation takes away that fluidity, and the two layers rub (CAD lecture).","Same idea as a pleural friction rub, when the visceral and parietal pleura are inflamed (for example, pleurisy).","How to listen","Stethoscope at the lower left sternal border.","Patient leaning forward (brings the heart closer to the chest wall).","Ask the patient to hold their breath to eliminate breath sounds and focus on the heart.","From the CAD lecture: if the grating disappears when the patient holds their breath, it is not cardiac in origin (it is respiratory)."]},{"s":"Complications: Pericardial Effusion & Tamponade▾","a":"complications-pericardial-effusion-tamponade","k":["Complications: Pericardial Effusion & Tamponade▾","Pericardial Effusion","Fluid in the pericardial space","Pericardial effusion is to the pericardium what pleural effusion is to the pleural space: third spacing, a collection of fluid that serves absolutely no purpose.","Symptoms come from the fluid squeezing the heart.","Cues: cough, tachypnea, difficulty breathing, and heart sounds that aren't crisp (fluid between the stethoscope and the heart).","The pericardial space doesn't expand much. As it fills, the heart gets squished, can't contract and release like it should, and cardiac output suffers.","Seen on an echocardiogram (or TEE), which delineates the heart's structures.","Cardiac Tamponade","Tamponade is time-sensitive and can be life-threatening. It develops when a pericardial effusion increases in volume.","Dyspnea, angina, restlessness, anxiety — Listed manifestations.","Decreased cardiac output (changes in vital signs) — Blood pressure is an indirect measure of cardiac output.","Marked jugular venous distension — Backup of fluid from the tamponade.","Very muffled heart sounds — Fluid around the heart.","Narrowed pulse pressure — See below.","Pulsus paradoxus (a classic) — See below.","Narrowed pulse pressure","Pulse pressure = systolic minus diastolic.","120/80 gives a pulse pressure of 40: pretty average.","In tamponade it narrows, such as 110/90: a pulse pressure of 20.","Pulsus paradoxus","BP normally drops a little with breathing, but not significantly. Significant = greater than 10 mmHg.","Systolic BP is 10 mmHg or more higher on expiration than on inspiration. (Lewis says it one way, UpToDate the other: 10 mmHg lower on inspiration. Same thing.)","For the skilled practitioner; the instructor has assessed it when the patient has an arterial line (systolic waxes and wanes with breathing). You won't get checked off on it.","1. Inflate the cuff beyond a palpable BP — You are palpating, not auscultating as in a normal BP.","2. Deflate gradually; note when sounds are first audible on expiration only — First number.","3. Note when sounds are audible on both inspiration and expiration — Second number.","4. A difference greater than 10 indicates tamponade — Lewis has a table, and there is a video on Canvas."]},{"s":"Diagnostics▾","a":"diagnostics","k":["Diagnostics▾","EKG — Diffuse, widespread ST elevation in almost all leads. ST elevation in only two contiguous leads points to an acute MI instead.","Echocardiogram — Determines whether pericardial effusion or cardiac tamponade has occurred.","WBC, sed rate, CRP — May be elevated (it's an \"-itis\"). Sed rate and CRP are non-specific inflammation markers: sed rate more chronic, CRP more acute, and neither tells where the inflammation is.","ST elevation in an MI is covered in CAD & ACS"]},{"s":"Treatment▾","a":"treatment","k":["Treatment▾","Bed rest, head of bed at 45 degrees — Plus measures to decrease anxiety.","Antibiotics — Only if bacteria is the causative agent; otherwise not indicated.","NSAIDs (the hallmark treatment) — Good old-fashioned NSAIDs: naproxen or high-dose ibuprofen (both over the counter). No morphine or opioids, because the problem is inflammation. Diagnosing it matters, because treatment differs from, say, p…","Colchicine — For recurrent pericarditis (covered again with arthritis and gout).","Prednisone taper — Only if NSAIDs are ineffective.","Pericardiocentesis (emergency) — Pericardiocentesis is to the pericardium as thoracentesis is to the pleural space: a needle removes the fluid. Per the lecture, removing about 5 to 10 mL can increase stroke volume by as much as 50%, letting…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-endocarditis.html","t":"Infective Endocarditis","secs":[{"s":"Infective Endocarditis","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 5","What It Is","Subacute vs Acute","Who Gets It & What Causes It","Vegetations & Emboli","Manifestations","History & Diagnostic Tests","Prophylactic Antibiotics","Treatment & Nursing Care","Self-Test Flashcards","With pericarditis think inflammation; with endocarditis think infection. \"Infective endocarditis\" is the longer name, but it is usually just called endocarditis."]},{"s":"What It Is▾","a":"what-it-is","k":["What It Is▾","The endocardium and the valves","The endocardium is the inside of the heart, which is a cue to where the problem is.","The valves are a very movable part of the heart, and a lot of the manifestations of endocarditis relate to the valves.","Endocarditis occurs when blood flow within the heart lets the organism infect previously damaged valves or another endothelial surface."]},{"s":"Subacute vs Acute▾","a":"subacute-vs-acute","k":["Subacute vs Acute▾","Subacute","Affects those with pre-existing heart problems.","Typically milder in presentation.","Can be more chronic.","Acute","Affects healthy valves.","Manifests itself quicker.","Therapy needs to be prompt and vigorous."]},{"s":"Who Gets It & What Causes It▾","a":"who-gets-it-what-causes-it","k":["Who Gets It & What Causes It▾","Two things have to happen","A history of damaged epithelium, such as a valve replacement or a defect.","A port of entry for infection to reach the endocardium: a sore, a dental procedure, IV drug use.","Causative organisms","Most common: Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus viridans (bacterial).","Other possible pathogens: fungi and viruses, so not all endocarditis is bacterial."]},{"s":"Vegetations & Emboli▾","a":"vegetations-emboli","k":["Vegetations & Emboli▾","Why vegetations matter","Infection on the moving valves develops vegetations, for example mitral valve vegetation between the left atrium and left ventricle. Bacterial endocarditis of the mitral valve covers it with large, irregular vegetations.","The valves are constantly opening and closing, so vegetations can slough off and become emboli, dispersed into the vascular system.","Left-sided vegetation","Leaves the heart into the organs.","Can cause a limb infarction or an infarction in any organ of the body.","Right-sided vegetation","Blood from the right side goes to the lungs.","Like a pulmonary embolus."]},{"s":"Manifestations▾","a":"manifestations","k":["Manifestations▾","Very non-specific at first","Timing: the transcript says it typically occurs \"within 10 two weeks\" of the infectious organism (unclear wording in the transcript, so no firm number is given here).","Low-grade fever, chills, malaise, fatigue, anorexia, joint pain, muscle pain. If you didn't know better, you'd think it was the flu.","Clubbing of the fingers is the odd one out, and it comes later in the disease process, not early.","Findings Unique to Endocarditis","The instructor says to commit these to memory: \"you don't hear about these in any other condition,\" and they \"would be great NCLEX questions.\"","Splinter hemorrhages — A longitudinal streak in the nail bed. — Vascular manifestation: vegetations leaving the left side of the heart and going to the periphery.","Petechiae — Little red hemorrhagic spots; smooth, flat (a macule), not raised, not painful. — Micro-emboli of vegetation. Fingers, eye, mouth, ankles, antecubital and popliteal areas.","Osler nodes — Tender papules on the fingertips and toes; look like they have a little pus in them. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive of (specific to) infective endocarditis.","Janeway lesions — Flat, macular, painless red spots on the palms and soles. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive.","Roth spots — Little hemorrhagic areas in the back of the retina. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive.","Telling the two apart: Osler nodes are tender (fingertips and toes); Janeway lesions are painless and flat (palms and soles).","Clinical Manifestations","New-onset murmur — A murmur is about the opening and closing of the valve. You should hear S1 and S2 crisply; damaged valves produce a murmur, an adventitious heart sound graded from 1 to 6.","Heart failure — The most common complication of infective endocarditis. Damaged valves leak blood back instead of letting it go one direction, the blood backs up, and the patient develops left- and right-sided heart failure."]},{"s":"History & Diagnostic Tests▾","a":"history-diagnostic-tests","k":["History & Diagnostic Tests▾","History — Recent dental, urological, or gynecologic procedure; IV drug use.","Blood cultures — Three cultures drawn over an hour apart from three different sites; positive results are indicative of endocarditis.","Echocardiogram — Evidence of endocardial enlargement."]},{"s":"Prophylactic Antibiotics▾","a":"prophylactic-antibiotics","k":["Prophylactic Antibiotics▾","Who gets prophylaxis","A mechanical heart valve.","A history of endocarditis.","A history of congenital heart disease.","A heart transplant.","Before which procedures","Any dental visit.","A biopsy of any kind.","Tonsil and adenoid removal.","A wound infection or urinary tract infection."]},{"s":"Treatment & Nursing Care▾","a":"treatment-nursing-care","k":["Treatment & Nursing Care▾","Treatment","Typically hospitalized initially; medications usually given IV.","Complete eradication may take weeks upon weeks; relapses are common.","Home care may include going home with a PICC line for IV antibiotics.","Nursing care and teaching","Identify and educate high-risk individuals so they can avoid infections.","Good rest; good oral hygiene (the mouth is where bacteria can enter).","Let the provider know before any dental procedure.","Refer IV drug users to drug rehab.","Monitor for the non-specific signs and symptoms that indicate a recurrence.","Endocarditis as a cause of valve regurgitation comes up again — review it in Valvular Heart Disease"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-valvular-heart-disease.html","t":"Valvular Heart Disease","secs":[{"s":"Valvular Heart Disease","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 6","Valve Review","Types of Valve Problems","Manifestations","Conservative Management","Repairing the Valve","Replacing the Valve: Mechanical vs Biologic","After Valve Replacement","Self-Test Flashcards","The last of the three inflammatory, infective, and valvular voiceovers. The instructor says you really need to know your valves: where they are and what they do."]},{"s":"Valve Review▾","a":"valve-review","k":["Valve Review▾","What the valves do","Valves let blood leave one chamber and go into the next. When the next chamber contracts, the valve slams shut, keeping blood moving in a unidirectional path. Stenosis, prolapse, or regurgitation messes with that and causes backflow.","AV valves: tricuspid and mitral. Semilunar valves: pulmonic and aortic.","The mitral lies between the left atrium and left ventricle; the aortic lies after the left ventricle, before systemic circulation.","Left heart valves take more of a beating, because the left side is the high-pressure system. A repaired or replaced valve is, more times than not, the mitral or the aortic.","Auscultation Landmarks","Aortic — Right sternal border, 2nd ICS — S2 heard best (the base of the heart).","Pulmonic — Left sternal border, 2nd ICS","Erb's point — Left sternal border, 3rd ICS — S1 and S2 both heard best simultaneously in one place.","Tricuspid — 4th ICS","Mitral — 5th ICS, left midclavicular line — S1 heard best (the apex; closure of the mitral valve)."]},{"s":"Types of Valve Problems▾","a":"types-of-valve-problems","k":["Types of Valve Problems▾","Stenosis — The door that won't open all the way. — Constriction or narrowing; blood backs up. — Often a history of rheumatic heart disease.","Regurgitation — The door that won't close all the way. — An incompetent valve; blood backs up into the previous chamber when it shouldn't. — Older age, or infective endocarditis.","Prolapse — The leaflets buckle. — Etiology unknown. The most common valve problem in the US; often seen in younger people and may improve with age.","Infective endocarditis damages the valves — review it in Infective Endocarditis"]},{"s":"Manifestations▾","a":"manifestations","k":["Manifestations▾","They start to look like heart failure","Lewis has a long list; you don't need to memorize all of it. Stick with the classic ones below.","Regardless of the cause, blood backs up, and the patient shows left and right heart failure.","Backup from the right side goes to the periphery; backup from the left side shows up in the lungs.","Classic manifestations: exertional dyspnea, weakness, fatigue, possibly chest pain, maybe a murmur."]},{"s":"Conservative Management▾","a":"conservative-management","k":["Conservative Management▾","Initially we try not to do surgery; treatment escalates if the patient becomes symptomatic enough.","Prophylactic antibiotics as necessary — To prevent rheumatic fever, rheumatic heart disease, and infective endocarditis.","Sodium restriction — \"Salt sucks and water follows salt\": keep excess fluid off board.","Heart failure medications — To treat and control the heart failure that has resulted from the valve problem.","Anticoagulants and/or antidysrhythmics — May be ordered."]},{"s":"Repairing the Valve▾","a":"repairing-the-valve","k":["Repairing the Valve▾","If surgery is needed, we would rather repair the patient's own valve than replace it. Percutaneous options include a commissurotomy, a transluminal balloon valvuloplasty, or a percutaneous valve replacement (such as an aortic valve replacem…","Commissurotomy — \"-otomy\" means opening: the provider opens up a stenosed valve.","Balloon valvuloplasty — For a stiff valve: a balloon is inflated to open it up. Like a commissurotomy, the goal is to open a valve that isn't as open as it should be, without replacing it.","Annuloplasty — Surgery to repair and reshape the valve, using annuloplasty rings so it works better."]},{"s":"Replacing the Valve: Mechanical vs Biologic▾","a":"replacing-the-valve-mechanical-vs-biologic","k":["Replacing the Valve: Mechanical vs Biologic▾","Mechanical valve","More durable; lasts longer.","Needs lifetime anticoagulation: the body sees the mechanical pieces as foreign, and clots might form. That is the disadvantage.","For someone who will be compliant with lifetime anticoagulation.","You will hear a click (it is metal). Some say you can hear it from the bedside without a stethoscope.","Biologic valve (usually porcine, a pig valve)","Less durable; doesn't last as long (less than 8 to 10 years).","No need for anticoagulation.","For someone older with a shorter life expectancy, or someone unwilling or unable to adhere to anticoagulation.","No click; you hear only the closure of the valve.","Adventitious heart sounds so far: a rub, a murmur, a gallop, and now a click. Who has a click? Someone with a mechanical valve."]},{"s":"After Valve Replacement▾","a":"after-valve-replacement","k":["After Valve Replacement▾","Recovery and anticoagulation","Recovery is similar to a CABG; sometimes valves are replaced at the same time as the bypass (\"killing two birds with one stone\").","A prosthetic valve means anticoagulation. On warfarin (Coumadin), the INR is monitored for a lifetime.","Per the lecture: normal INR 0.8 to 1.1; goal INR on warfarin 2.5 to 3.5. Thin the blood, but not too thin; any higher and they could have issues.","Antidote for bleeding: vitamin K.","CABG recovery is the comparison point — review it in CAD & ACS","Eat vitamin K foods consistently (kale greens, for example); don't binge — Vitamin K messes with the clotting factors and reverses the effect of warfarin.","Electric razor instead of a straight-edge razor — Take measures not to bleed.","Monitor for any signs of excessive bleeding — Anticoagulated.","Avoid an MRI — The valve has metal.","Wear a medical alert bracelet or card — Always."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 7","The Lectures' Own Order","The Five Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It is a map of Week 1's five topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one as if it were unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate: it puts all five pa…"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order","k":["The Lectures' Own Order▾","Week 1 runs from the tests, to the most common problem (blocked coronary arteries), to the three inflammatory, infective, and valvular conditions. The instructor introduced them as one set: \"caring for the patient with inflammatory, infecti…","The Tools — How We Look at the Heart — Topic 2, CV Labs & Diagnostics — Serum markers (troponin for MI, BNP for heart failure, CRP and ESR for inflammation), then non-invasive tests (12-lead, tele, Holter, stress test, echo), then invasive …","The Blocked Artery — CAD to MI — Topic 3, CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome — A continuum: stable angina, unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI. Atherosclerosis causes almost all of it. Time is muscle: PCI within 90 minutes, thrombolytics within 30.","Outside the Heart — Inflammation — Topic 4, Pericarditis — The pericardium is inflamed; the friction rub is the hallmark. Its danger is fluid: effusion, then tamponade.","Inside the Heart — Infection — Topic 5, Infective Endocarditis — The endocardium and valves are infected; vegetations break off as emboli. Its most common complication is heart failure.","The Doors — Valves — Topic 6, Valvular Heart Disease — Stenosis (won't open), regurgitation (won't close), prolapse (buckles). All of them end up looking like heart failure."]},{"s":"The Five Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-five-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Five Topics at a Glance▾","2 — CV Labs & Diagnostics — Troponin is the most sensitive marker of myocardial damage (up in 2 to 3 hours, back in 10 to 14 days); a 12-lead can diagnose an MI but telemetry only monitors; only a left heart cath reaches the coronary arteri…","3 — CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome — Stable angina is predictable and relieved by rest or nitro; unstable angina, NSTEMI, and STEMI are ACS. One nitro without relief at home means call 911 and chew an aspirin; always check BP before nitro. D…","4 — Pericarditis — Inflammation of the pericardium: positional pain, better leaning forward, worse on inspiration, not relieved by nitro; friction rub at the lower left sternal border; diffuse ST elevation; treated with NSAIDs. Watch for ta…","5 — Infective Endocarditis — Needs damaged epithelium plus a port of entry; flu-like at first; splinter hemorrhages, petechiae, Osler nodes, Janeway lesions, and Roth spots are the findings to memorize; three blood cultures; weeks of IV ant…","6 — Valvular Heart Disease — Know your valves and landmarks; repair before replace; mechanical valves last longer but need lifetime anticoagulation and click, biologic valves need none but wear out sooner."]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere. If something feels like déjà vu, this table shows why.","Troponin — CV Labs & Diagnostics — \"Serum Cardiac Markers\" — CAD & ACS: it is what separates unstable angina (normal) from NSTEMI and STEMI (elevated), and it helps rule cardiac in or out with epigastric pain.","12-lead EKG vs telemetry — CV Labs & Diagnostics — \"Non-Invasive Procedures\" — CAD & ACS: a STEMI can't be diagnosed by tele; ST elevation on tele means get a 12-lead.","ST elevation — CAD & ACS — \"Diagnosing ACS: EKG Changes\" (two contiguous leads = MI) — Pericarditis: diffuse ST elevation in almost all leads.","Heart cath care and complications — CV Labs & Diagnostics — \"Invasive Procedures\" — CAD & ACS: PCI recovery is very similar to a heart cath.","CRP and sed rate — CV Labs & Diagnostics — \"Inflammation Markers\" — Pericarditis: may be elevated, since it is an \"-itis.\"","Echocardiogram — CV Labs & Diagnostics — Pericarditis (checks for effusion and tamponade) and Infective Endocarditis (endocardial enlargement).","Pericarditis as an MI complication; the friction rub — Pericarditis — \"The Pericardial Friction Rub\" — CAD & ACS: pericarditis a couple of days after an MI.","Pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade — Pericarditis — \"Complications\" — CV Labs & Diagnostics (poking the pericardium during a cath) and CAD & ACS (after CABG).","Heart failure — Its own content comes later in the course (\"we will talk about heart failure soon\"). — CAD & ACS (MI complication; crackles), Infective Endocarditis (most common complication), Valvular Heart Disease (what valve problems loo…","Murmurs — Infective Endocarditis — new-onset murmur, graded 1 to 6 — CAD & ACS (papillary muscle dysfunction) and Valvular Heart Disease (rub, murmur, gallop, click).","Prophylactic antibiotics — Infective Endocarditis — \"Prophylactic Antibiotics\" — Valvular Heart Disease: preventing rheumatic fever, rheumatic heart disease, and endocarditis.","Anticoagulation and bleeding — Valvular Heart Disease — warfarin, INR, vitamin K — CV Labs and CAD & ACS: heparin during caths and PCI, dual antiplatelets, bleeding after thrombolytics."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the five pages, condensed. Click through to a topic for the full explanation.","CV Labs & Diagnostics — Telemetry cannot diagnose; get a 12-lead. After a cath: watch for bleeding and hematoma, hold pressure, bed rest with the leg straight and head of bed below 30 degrees after a femoral approach, check distal pulses, c…","CAD & ACS — One nitro without relief at home: call 911 and chew an aspirin. Check BP before every nitro. Never combine nitro with ED drugs; never defibrillate over a nitro patch. 12-lead within 10 minutes of arrival. A rising heart rate aft…","Pericarditis — Cardiac tamponade is time-sensitive and life-threatening: muffled heart sounds, JVD, narrowed pulse pressure, pulsus paradoxus. Emergency pericardiocentesis.","Infective Endocarditis — Acute endocarditis needs prompt, vigorous therapy. Prophylactic antibiotics before dental work and other listed procedures for high-risk patients. Vegetations can embolize: left side to organs and limbs, right side …","Valvular Heart Disease — Mechanical valve: lifetime anticoagulation and INR monitoring; vitamin K is the antidote; consistent vitamin K intake; electric razor; no MRI; medical alert bracelet."]}]}];
